# Target C-II — the strengthened SIGNED braid propagates at quarter-power scale through the common-cut grid

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only corollary of the bounded-noncrossing-degree upgrade and the SIGNED common-cut robust-owner-grid theorem; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** SIGNED / COMMON-CUT side of the 15-spaced one-sign strong-TB1 Horn upper family through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** bounded noncrossing degree; SIGNED common-cut robust owner grid; row-slot stabilization; diagonal Ramsey bound; SIGNED common fence-cell reduction  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_signed_quarter_power_propagation_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current common-cut SIGNED theorem was written with a starting crossing braid

\[
n_D=D^{\varepsilon_K-o(1)},
\]

because it inherited the earlier Ramsey extraction. The bounded-noncrossing-degree theorem removes that loss. In the same COMMON-CUT scaffold one may now start with

\[
\boxed{
n_D=D^{1/4-o(1)}
}
\]

pairwise-properly-crossing SIGNED chords, with distinct 15-spaced Horn owners and all previous metric tags unchanged.

Every subsequent step of the robust-owner-grid proof is hereditary and uses only constant-factor thinning, `O_K(1)` directional deletions, or the row-slot/Ramsey arguments. The entire normal form therefore strengthens quantitatively.

The immediate consequences are:

1. the far-owner braid and the robust marker-row family both have size `D^(1/4-o(1))`;
2. after the ROW-UNIQUE / COLUMN-UNIQUE split, the surviving family still has size `D^(1/4-o(1))`;
3. COLUMN-UNIQUE contains `D^(1/4-o(1))` pairwise-crossing SIGNED chords with one common exact `B_0` fence-cell type;
4. ROW-UNIQUE admits a parameterized one-sided-cable extraction: for every fixed `0<a<1/2`, one obtains
   \[
   D^{(1-2a)/4-o(1)}
   \]
   robust co-phased rows and
   \[
   \left({a\over8}-o(1)\right)\log_2D
   \]
   pairwise-crossing SIGNED chords whose every mutual crossing lies on the same side of the whole row slab.

For the old choice `a=1/4`, this becomes

\[
\boxed{D^{1/8-o(1)}\text{ robust rows}}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{\left({1\over32}-o(1)\right)\log_2D\text{ one-sided cable chords}.}
\]

A balanced choice `a=1/3` gives a pre-Ramsey chord set and ordered row block both of scale `D^(1/12-o(1))`, followed by a cable of

\[
\left({1\over24}-o(1)\right)\log_2D.
\]

These replace the previous exponents proportional to the microscopic constant `epsilon_K`.

---

## 1. Quarter-power input survives the common-cut owner split

Let `F_D` be the stronger localized SIGNED braid from the bounded-noncrossing-degree theorem. On the COMMON-CUT side,

\[
|F_D|=n_D=D^{1/4-o(1)}.
\tag{QP.1}
\]

Every chord still has:

- one distinct retained strong-TB1 owner coordinate;
- the same common supported cut;
- the SIGNED projection
  \[
  r_e\cdot w_*
  \le-\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)D;
  \]
- length `D-O(D^-1)`;
- pairwise proper crossing and vertex-disjointness.

Repeat the owner split from the robust-grid theorem. After selecting one owner side of the common cut and dividing that side into a far half and a near marker half, there are families

\[
E_D^{far},\qquad A_D^{owner}
\]

with

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{far}|,|A_D^{owner}|\ge {n_D\over4}-O(1)
=D^{1/4-o(1)}.
}
\tag{QP.2}

Every far-owner chord supports every marker row.

Every marker row is adjacent to a retained strong-TB1 robust interface, so the existing global column family satisfies

\[
\boxed{|B_0|>0.0783D-o(D)}
\tag{QP.3}

and

\[
A_D^{owner}\times B_0
\]

is one inverse-`D` near-equilateral train grid.

No new averaging is used.

---

## 2. The uniqueness fork remains quarter-power

The common-cut theorem deletes only `O_K(1)` chord directions near the row-sector boundaries and then keeps one of two direction classes. Applying exactly the same step to (QP.2) gives either

### ROW-UNIQUE

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{R}|=D^{1/4-o(1)},
}
\tag{QP.4}

with every chord meeting every row in `A_D^{owner}` exactly once; or

### COLUMN-UNIQUE

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{C}|=D^{1/4-o(1)},
}
\tag{QP.5}

with every chord meeting every train in the fixed linear family `B_0` at most once.

The COLUMN-UNIQUE fence-cell proof is purely hereditary after this point. Hence its Helly cell `C_g` and the four exact types

```text
THROUGH
LEFT-ANCHORED
RIGHT-ANCHORED
CELL-INTERNAL
```

may be chosen on a sub-braid of size

\[
\boxed{D^{1/4-o(1)}}.
\tag{QP.6}

Thus the entire fixed-cell obstruction is quarter-power, not `D^(epsilon_K-o(1))`.

---

## 3. Re-optimize the ROW-UNIQUE row-slot argument

Assume ROW-UNIQUE. Let

\[
H_D:=|A_D^{owner}|=(1/4+o(1))n_D
\]

up to harmless lower-bound notation. Fix any constant

\[
0<a<{1\over2}
\tag{QP.7}
\]

and retain

\[
k_D:=\lfloor n_D^a\rfloor
\tag{QP.8}
\]

ROW-UNIQUE chords.

There are

\[
C_D={k_D\choose2}=n_D^{2a+o(1)}
\tag{QP.9}
\]

possible chord-pair crossing slots. The existing row-slot stabilization lemma gives an ordered row subblock satisfying

\[
|A_D^{ord}|
\ge {H_D-C_D\over C_D+1}.
\tag{QP.10}
\]

Because `a<1/2`, `C_D=o(n_D)`, and therefore

\[
\boxed{
|A_D^{ord}|
=n_D^{1-2a-o(1)}
=D^{(1-2a)/4-o(1)}.
}
\tag{QP.11}

On all rows in `A_D^{ord}`, the `k_D` chord intersections have one fixed total order. Hence every pairwise chord crossing lies outside the closed slab bounded by its extreme rows.

Color each chord pair by which exterior side contains its unique crossing. The standard bound

\[
R(r,r)<4^{r-1}
\]

gives a monochromatic sub-braid of size

\[
{1\over2}\log_2 k_D-O(1).
\tag{QP.12}

Now

\[
\log_2 n_D
=\left({1\over4}-o(1)\right)\log_2D,
\]

so (QP.8)--(QP.12) give

\[
\boxed{
|G_D|
\ge
\left({a\over8}-o(1)\right)\log_2D.
}
\tag{QP.13}

Every mutual crossing of `G_D` lies on the same side of the entire row slab (QP.11). Inside the slab the chord pieces form one ordered noncrossing cable.

---

## 4. Useful concrete choices

### Old quarter choice: `a=1/4`

Then

\[
k_D=D^{1/16-o(1)},
\]

\[
\boxed{|A_D^{ord}|=D^{1/8-o(1)}},
\tag{QP.14}

and

\[
\boxed{
|G_D|\ge\left({1\over32}-o(1)\right)\log_2D.
}
\tag{QP.15}

### Balanced polynomial dimensions: `a=1/3`

Then the pre-Ramsey chord population and stabilized row block have the same exponent:

\[
\boxed{
k_D=D^{1/12-o(1)}},
\qquad
\boxed{|A_D^{ord}|=D^{1/12-o(1)}}.
\tag{QP.16}

The final one-sided cable has

\[
\boxed{
|G_D|\ge\left({1\over24}-o(1)\right)\log_2D.
}
\tag{QP.17}

More generally (QP.11) and (QP.13) expose the full row-height/cable-width tradeoff.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The common-cut SIGNED branch now has the following scale.

```text
starting localized obstruction:
    D/log D SIGNED inactive near-diameters;

crossing extraction:
    D^(1/4-o(1)) vertex-disjoint pairwise-crossing SIGNED braid;

owner synchronization:
    D^(1/4-o(1)) far-owner braid
    x D^(1/4-o(1)) robust co-phased owner rows
    x one fixed >0.0783D train family B_0;

ROW-UNIQUE:
    for every 0<a<1/2,
    D^((1-2a)/4-o(1)) ordered rows
    plus (a/8-o(1)) log_2 D one-sided cable chords;

COLUMN-UNIQUE:
    D^(1/4-o(1)) crossing chords in one exact common B_0 fence cell/type.
```

The remaining issue is metric/order closure inside these normal forms. The earlier tiny `epsilon_K` is no longer a meaningful limitation on the SIGNED branch.

**Disposition:** propagated the bounded-noncrossing-degree improvement through the current COMMON-CUT robust-grid, row-cable, and fence-cell reductions and re-optimized the row-slot tradeoff. C-II and Level I remain open.
